\chapter{The Workstation on the Engineer's Desk}
\label{ch:workstations}
\index{workstations}\index{Sun Microsystems}\index{Apollo}\index{Silicon Graphics}

\section{Personal computing without surrendering the network}

A workstation was not simply a small server with a monitor attached.
Its appeal lay in combining local computational power, a high-resolution
display, networking, and a serious software environment for one technical
user. It changed the relationship between individual and institution.
An engineer could control a responsive local machine while still sharing
files, printers, mail, and compute resources with colleagues.

The term covers a varied market. Sun systems made Unix and Ethernet a
particularly visible combination. Silicon Graphics emphasized advanced
graphics and visualization. Hewlett-Packard, IBM, DEC, and others supplied
technical systems with their own architectures and Unix variants.
Apollo's influential Domain environment was not initially a conventional
Unix system, although later products incorporated Unix compatibility and
services. Treating every workstation as an identical Unix box erases the
competition that made the category interesting.

Nor did the workstation eliminate time-sharing. A local machine could act
as a window onto a remote host, execute some tasks itself, and submit
larger work to a shared system. The new pattern distributed responsibility
instead of simply replacing central computing. Network transparency became
an attractive promise because users wanted resources to feel close even
when the hardware was elsewhere.

\section{Why Unix fit the market}

Unix brought compilers, editors, a shell, file tools, and an established
body of technical software. C made source-level portability plausible
across changing processors. Universities had already trained people who
knew the environment. Vendors could offer a machine that felt familiar to
these users while differentiating through performance, graphics,
peripherals, and support.

This was a favorable division of labor, but not a guarantee of effortless
portability. A program could depend on byte order, word size, undocumented
library behavior, or a particular window toolkit. Binary compatibility was
harder still: an executable built for a Motorola processor could not
simply run on a SPARC or MIPS processor. Unix provided a common vocabulary
for ports, not a universal executable format for every workstation.

Vendor systems consequently mixed common practice with proprietary
investment. SunOS, HP-UX, AIX, IRIX, and other systems supplied distinct
administration tools, graphics stacks, drivers, and commercial applications.
Their developers were solving real hardware and customer problems.
Fragmentation was partly the result of legitimate specialization, even
when it also served commercial lock-in.

Libes and Ressler's \emph{Life with UNIX} captures the variety and
enthusiasm of this environment. Its own production note is telling:
the book was composed with FrameMaker on a Sun workstation and prepared
for a phototypesetter through PostScript. Unix publishing had expanded
from a local formatter and device into an ecosystem of commercial
applications and interchangeable output paths \parencite{life}.

\section{Shared files, shared assumptions}
\index{NFS}\index{NIS}

A networked desktop needs shared state. Sun's Network File System, first
released in the mid-1980s, made remote files accessible through familiar
filesystem operations. A mounted directory could refer to data on another
machine. Existing applications could often use that data without learning
a new explicit file-transfer workflow.

The convenience concealed difficult semantics. Local file operations
already have failure cases, but remote operations add unavailable servers,
network partitions, retransmissions, and inconsistent caches. Early NFS's
stateless server design simplified certain aspects of crash recovery,
while shifting other responsibilities to clients and auxiliary services.
Later NFS versions changed the balance. ``NFS is stateless'' is therefore
an observation about particular versions, not a permanent definition.

A laboratory also needed common user identities and configuration.
Directory and naming services could save administrators from maintaining
every machine independently. They could also propagate a mistaken entry
or an overly trusting security assumption across a whole site. Shared
convenience enlarged the consequences of a shared error.

Diskless workstations made the dependence especially visible. A machine
could have a powerful processor and a personal display while relying on
network services to boot and obtain its root filesystem. Personal
interaction did not imply local autonomy. An outage in an apparently
remote administrative layer could stop an entire room of individual desks.

\section{Graphics makes the machine a working instrument}

Computer-aided design, scientific visualization, animation, and engineering
analysis needed more than character terminals. A bitmap display could show
multiple windows, diagrams, and images. Specialized graphics hardware
could accelerate transformations and rendering. Unix processes could
produce data that a graphical application interpreted in ways no line
printer could represent effectively.

Silicon Graphics illustrates the relationship between applications and
system design. Graphics workloads encouraged attention to pipelines,
geometry, memory bandwidth, synchronization, and display hardware.
IRIX was part of a product whose value depended on the entire machine
working well for demanding visual tasks. A kernel benchmark alone could
not describe that value.

At the same time, workstation graphics was not one settled architecture.
Some environments used proprietary window systems; others adopted X;
toolkits and desktop conventions competed. The user could encounter a
common operating-system ancestry through an interface with radically
different menus, fonts, and interaction rules. Later chapters explain why
X deliberately left many of those choices outside the display server.

The workstation also exposed a persistent distinction between technical
and everyday usability. An expert might value remote execution, programmable
configuration, and unusual window-management behavior. A new user might
find the same environment inconsistent and exhausting. Both reactions
could be reasonable. A product designed for one audience can be successful
without being a model for every audience.

\section{RISC, price, and the changing competitive boundary}
\index{RISC}

Many workstation vendors adopted RISC processors to pursue high performance
with architectures that suited contemporary compiler and chip-design
strategies. SPARC, MIPS, PA-RISC, and POWER belong to this broad setting,
although their designs and commercial histories differ. Unix's portability
helped vendors move software toward new processors without rebuilding the
entire programming environment from nothing.

The surrounding market changed. Commodity personal-computer components
became faster and cheaper. Windows NT offered a different technical and
commercial platform. Linux and BSD systems brought Unix-like environments
to inexpensive machines. Graphics acceleration moved into mass-market
products, supported by a much larger gaming and consumer market.

This did not make specialized workstations suddenly useless. Professional
certification, applications, drivers, support contracts, and installed
workflows remained valuable. It did change which parts of a machine could
justify a premium. A vendor competing through a proprietary processor,
operating system, graphics subsystem, and enclosure now faced competitors
benefiting from huge production volumes elsewhere.

The decline of the classic Unix workstation market should therefore not be
confused with the disappearance of workstation practice. Engineers still
use powerful local machines connected to shared services. Many now use
Linux, macOS, or Windows with Unix tools. The packaging changed more
completely than the pattern of work.

\section{What survived the workstation era}

The workstation helped normalize several expectations: a technical user
should have a responsive graphical environment, network access should be
ordinary, remote resources should be reachable from local applications,
and a development toolchain should be part of the platform. These
expectations outlived many of the vendors that established them.

It also created a body of engineering whose later influence was not
limited to Unix-branded products. Networking stacks, graphics APIs,
compiler work, filesystem techniques, and window-system experience moved
between institutions and systems. NeXT's workstation environment would
have a particularly consequential afterlife in Apple's software platform.
Mach and BSD would survive there in a form very different from a
university terminal room.

A familiar historical temptation is to describe the era as the last time
computers were coherent and understandable. The contemporary complaints
collected in \emph{The UNIX-HATERS Handbook} resist that nostalgia.
Users already struggled with window systems, inconsistent interfaces,
network assumptions, and administration \parencite{haters}.
What looks coherent from a distance often felt like a collection of
unfinished agreements to the person trying to make it work.

Those agreements were about to become the subject of explicit commercial
and standards conflict. If many vendors offered Unix, who could define
what Unix meant? Which interfaces should applications rely on, and who
controlled the name? The workstation's success made these questions more
valuable, and therefore harder to settle.
